From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 06:26:35 +0200
Subject: Preserve an @internal.diagnostic candidate's errors as the
 search-failure message

Squashed from 3 commits, oldest first:

* Preserve diagnostics from `@internal.diagnostic` implicit candidates

A given marked `@scala.annotation.internal.diagnostic` whose macro aborts
while tried as an implicit candidate now fails the search normally, but its
reported errors become the authoritative message if the overall search
fails, bypassing `@implicitNotFound`. This lets a diagnostic catch-all
(Frontier) abort with a rendered search tree without corrupting `NotGiven`,
`summonFrom` or default `using` arguments.

Enabled by `-Z:diagnostic-givens`; without it, upstream's code runs at every site this
patch touches.

* Ship diagnostic in the supplementary proscala-library jar

The standard library additions of the fork live under library-proscala/src and
are built into their own jar (proscala-library, plus a Scala.js variant), so the
scala-library and scalajs-scalalib jars are byte-identical to what upstream
builds from the same tree. The compiler looks the class up by name and runs
without it.

* Do not invert a diagnostic candidate's failure under NotGiven

`NotGiven[X]` is resolved by inverting each candidate's result, and an
`@internal.diagnostic` catch-all is itself a candidate for `NotGiven[X]`.
Its failure said nothing about `X`, yet it was inverted into a success
before `NotGiven`'s own instances were reached, so `NotGiven[X]` held for
every `X` (propensive/soundness#2123). A failing candidate that carries
the annotation is now left as a failure. The failure's type is not the
test: when `X` is absent, the nested diagnostic failure propagates into
`amb1`'s failure, which must still invert.

Written with Claude Code; the change and tests were reviewed and run
locally by the author.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: searchdiag
